  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nThe key observation is that the optimal solution lies in a low-dimensional manifold of Toeplitz-constant functions. Traditional gradient-based methods get stuck in local optima, while binary representations are too restrictive. We overcome this by using a combination of two novel ideas:\n\n1. A coarse-grid initialization that captures the majority-structure of the optimal function\n2. Progressive resolution refinement using a dual-solver approach\n\nThe mathematical basis is recognizing that the overlap integral for a step function h and its shift 1-h can be expressed as:\n\nC(k) = \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nWe minimize the maximum C(k) while maintaining the integral constraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1.\n\nThe critical bottleneck is the non-convex optimization landscape, which we address by using a hierarchy of representations and a multi-resolution optimization strategy that balances exploration and refinement.\n\nMathematical specification:\n\n1. We represent h as a piecewise constant function with n = 512 samples over [0,2] (dx = 2/512)\n\n2. The optimization seeks to minimize:\n   C = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\n3. Constraint:\n   \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 1   \u2192  sum(h) * dx = 1\n\n4. The solution is expressed through:\n   - A multi-resolution optimization loop\n   - A gradient-based refinement stage\n   - Careful projection to maintain integral constraint\n\nImplementation:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - If initial_h_values provided, downsample to 128 samples and upscale to 512\n   - Else: Generate coarse solution (n=128) using projected gradient descent\n   - Set target_sum = n_original/2 (exactly, as floating point for projection)\n\n2. Coarse-grid (n=128) initialization:\n   - Run projected gradient descent with momentum to find initial rough solution\n   - This captures the majority structure with minimal computation\n   - Project solution to satisfy integral constraint\n\n3. Progressive resolution refinement:\n   - Grid doubling (from 128 to 256, then 512) with correction steps\n   - At each resolution, maintain parent feasibility by projection\n   - Use constraint-compatible gradient computation via FFT\n\n4. Parallel multi-start local search:\n   - After each resolution doubling, launch M=4 optimization instances\n   - Each instance uses:\n        * Shared global gradient estimate\n        * Resolution-safe constraints\n        * Frozen high-frequency structure\n\n5. Final optimization:\n   - After reaching n=512, run gradient descent with momentum\n   - Project gradient onto constraint manifold\n   - Compute exact constraint projection using iterative method\n\n6. Search for convergence:\n   - Track best solution across all resolutions\n   - Terminate when improvement < 1e-6 or time < 100 seconds\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\n1. Coarse grid size: 128 points (minimal to capture structure)\n2. Multi-start instances: 4 (ensures diversity)\n3. Final resolution: n=512 (definitive solution)\n4. Momentum coefficient: 0.9\n5. Target integral: 256 (for n=512)\n\nNumerical robustness:\n\n1. Memory: Two FFT workspaces of size for n=512 (complex numbers)\n2. Timing: 20,000 iterations in main loop\n3. Projection: Iterative box projection with: \n   - Initial guess: Current solution\n   - Max iterations: 50\n   - Tolerance: 1e-8\n\nRuntimes safeguards:\n\n1. Time tracking:\n   - Budget: 1100 seconds\n   - Graceful degradation on timeout\n\n2. Feasibility management:\n   - After each operation, project to feasible set\n   - Save best solution at each resolution\n\n3. Objective evaluation:\n   - Use FFT-based correlation:\n        N_points = target function length\n        C = max( np.correlate(h.astype(float), 1-h.astype(float), mode='full' ) * (2.0/N_points)\n\n4. Gradient-free alternative for early stages:\n   - Use finite differences for local windows\n\nRunt implementation details:\n\n1. Project function-safe operations by:\n   - Using separate helper for FFT projection\n   - Maintaining solution array as np.float64\n\n2. Temperature-like setting for momentum decay (not true cooling)\n\n3. Dimension management:\n   - resolution doublings: 2 (n=128 \u2192 n=512)\n   - Each doubling should take < 300 seconds\n\n4. Error handling:\n   - Always project to feasible region\n   - Use Try-Except for projection failures\n\n5. Fallback recovery:\n   - If budget exhausted, return best solution found\n   - Use minimal projection for broken solutions",
